      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the table. one thing i'm stuck on is the trading part. can u explain why mutual funds only trade at the end of the day? and do you think there are any hidden issues or risks with the way mutual fund prices are calculated compared to etfs?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mutual funds typically trade at the end of the day because they rely on a closed-end market, which doesn't observe real-time quotes. This is a historical convention dating back to the mid-19th century. The investment manager can only know who wants to buy and sell the fund shares when the market is open, and at this time, they trade through an exchange with other market participants.\n\nThere are some hidden issues with mutual fund pricing, particularly compared to ETFs, including:\n\n1. **Valuation timing**: Mutual funds may not reflect real-time market values when prices are set during the trading day. This can result in prices being quicker to trade based on ETF quotes.\n2. **No fractional shares on mutual funds**: Mutual funds typically don't offer fractional shares, which can make it difficult for investors to buy or sell small portions of a fund. This can lead to a \"lot\" system, where investors may be required to buy or sell larger blocks of shares.\n3. **Higher trading fees**: Mutual funds often charge higher trading fees, especially when late trades are submitted. These fees can eat into investors' returns over time.\n\nOverall, while the closed-end market structure is designed to facilitate trading, it can lead to faster trading on",
      "source": "target",
